# Look at the response headers: X-Instance, X-Cold-Start, X-Duration-Ms.
@base = http://localhost:5111
@alice = 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
@bob = 22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222
@carla = 33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333

############ Cold start, scaling out, scaling to zero ############

### 1st call: cold start (≈ 600 ms more). Run again right away: warm instance, a few ms.
GET {{base}}/accounts/{{alice}}

### Instance memory vs managed service: run several times, then wait 20 s (recycling) and run again
GET {{base}}/visits

############ Asynchronous processing through a queue ############

### The transfer is accepted (202) then executed by another function, triggered by the queue
POST {{base}}/transfers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "source": "{{alice}}", "target": "{{bob}}", "amount": 250 }

### Track the transfer (replace the identifier with the one returned above)
GET {{base}}/transfers/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Bob's notifications
GET {{base}}/accounts/{{bob}}/notifications

############ Fan-out: an uploaded file → one instance per transfer ############

### Upload a file of 8 transfers: ProcessImport splits it, ExecuteTransfer scales up to several instances
POST {{base}}/imports
Content-Type: text/plain

source;target;amount
33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333;11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111;10
33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333;22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222;20
33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333;11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111;30
33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333;22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222;40
33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333;11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111;50
33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333;22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222;60
33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333;11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111;70
33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333;22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222;80

### Statistics: look at ExecuteTransfer's "peakInstances"
GET {{base}}/platform/functions

############ Failures: retries and poison messages ############

### Simulate a failure of the text message provider (setting changed without redeploying)
PUT {{base}}/platform/settings/Sms:Down
Content-Type: application/json

{ "value": "true" }

### Make a transfer: it succeeds, but its notifications fail 3 times then go to "notifications-poison"
POST {{base}}/transfers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "source": "{{carla}}", "target": "{{alice}}", "amount": 5 }

### Queues: see notifications-poison (wait ~2 s)
GET {{base}}/platform/queues

### Repair, then replay the poison messages
PUT {{base}}/platform/settings/Sms:Down
Content-Type: application/json

{ "value": "false" }

###
POST {{base}}/platform/queues/notifications-poison/replay

############ Provider console ############

### Fire the timer right away (otherwise: every 30 s)
POST {{base}}/platform/timers/ProduceReport/fire

### Stored objects (imports and reports)
GET {{base}}/platform/storage

### Centralized log
GET {{base}}/platform/log?count=30

### Bill: per invocation and per GB-second
GET {{base}}/platform/bill
